WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.102 --> 00:00:10.220
"Thank you for subscribing to Vox. There are
a million of you! This is insane."

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.224
"I can't believe there are three million of
you." 

00:00:12.224 --> 00:00:13.620
"That's like one, two, three.."

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.751
"Four million!"

00:00:14.751 --> 00:00:17.015
"I can't believe there's eight million of
you out there."

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.600
So I want to shout out Tal Leming. He is the
artist behind the typeface that we have used

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.250
in literally every single one of our videos,
which is Balto.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:55.850
I wanted it to feel like a friend who knows
a lot about what they’re talking about because

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:59.680
that’s what typefaces do, you know, they
give the tone to the words.&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:05.080
Ben Marriot’s tutorials are very helpful and
I especially used his paper cutout tutorial

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.080
for many of my collage videos. The uterus
that I made.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:17.997
One internet resource I use a lot for visual
inspiration is designspiration, I hope that's

00:01:17.997 --> 00:01:19.427
you say it.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:27.710
I want to shout out Laurent Dury. He is a
composer on APM. I asked him if he would play

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:31.850
some music for me and he played a little jingle.
&nbsp;

00:01:33.647 --> 00:01:37.221
When you’re making the music are you ever
thinking about where it’s going to wind

00:01:37.221 --> 00:03:24.013
up? No, sometimes I’m feeling when writing,
“oh it could go to that type of documentary

00:01:44.734 --> 00:01:49.124
for example but most of the time I’m wrong.”

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:55.320
For a lot of our videos that I've worked on
we use David Rumsey’s map collection. And

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:01.340
it just makes it so easy to see how a region's
borders have been recorded through time.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:06.350
I'd love to shout out Geolayers and the maker
of Geolayers, which is this incredible software

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:11.630
that we use to animate all our maps. It’s
by far my favorite tool.&nbsp;

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:16.440
Rick Prelinger, who founded the Prelinger
Archives about like 20 years ago.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:22.670
There was no YouTube in the year 2000 and
it was really hard to see moving images online

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:25.350
and especially hard to see history online.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.660
You can tell that he’s thought about this
stuff for a really long time.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:33.280
We’ve only begun to scratch the surface
of what we can do with old sounds and images.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:40.160
A resource I use a ton is Newspapers.com.
It has thousands of newspapers going back

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.990
to I think like the 1700s sometimes.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:49.730
I use factbase a lot. It basically compiles
everything Donald Trump has ever said or tweeted

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.650
or written about, and they’re doing the
same for Biden.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:57.190
I want to shout out the Internet Archive.
You can get really beautiful high-res scans

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:04.020
of books and there’s really no alternative
online to being able to search TV captions.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:09.599
News programming in particular is required
by law in the United States to have closed

00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:14.730
captioning for deaf people and so the transcripts
you're seeing most of the time is the closed

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:18.840
captioning that we’ve pulled out of the
video stream and turned into a searchable

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:19.840
index.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.610
How many servers do you guys have?

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:24.013
Oh my gosh so many.
&nbsp;

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:33.320
I'd really like to shout out Connectedpapers.com.
You can type in one topic and it gives you a

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:39.319
specific paper and not just that paper but 
other research papers connected to that topic.&nbsp;

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:47.720
Worldradiohistory.com. You go to the website
and it's rough to look at but I have pretty

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:56.110
much used this website in every single music-related
video I've made probably since 2016.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:01.780
And I wanted to shout out the creators of
After Effects. They’re the people that changed,

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:06.560
you know, movie and tv graphics from being
these million dollar machines to something

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:08.019
you could do on a personal computer.&nbsp;

00:04:08.019 --> 00:04:12.060
We were trying to make something where you
could understand the pieces that you're fitting

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:15.280
together so that you can create

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.769
anything that you have in your mind.&nbsp;And
we were clearly at the right place at the

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:26.349
right time, we had no idea that there would
be this rolling wave of digital video adoption

00:04:26.349 --> 00:04:30.689
that has lasted 30 years now and is still
growing.&nbsp;

00:04:30.689 --> 00:04:38.309
There’s a plugin I use for After Effects
called Motion 2. It’s just like a workflow

00:04:38.309 --> 00:04:43.599
type plugin where there’s a lot of small
features added into this toolbar.

00:04:43.599 --> 00:04:50.789
So AEscripts.com is a library of scripts and
plugins that you can use over in After Effects.

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:55.139
And I think the most useful one is the layer
random shifter script.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.139 --> 00:05:01.490
I want to shout out the Rights and Licensing
team at Vox Media who keep us from how do

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:07.120
I say this? They keep us from doing things
in our videos that people would not like in

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.409
a law related sense hmm.

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:15.199
Yeah making sure that all the elements we
have in the video we are able to use, I think

00:05:15.199 --> 00:05:18.300
is probably the best way to put it.&nbsp;

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.009
Who on our team gives you the most grief?

00:05:20.009 --> 00:05:26.360
Nope. Not gonna do it. Not gonna call out
people, that’s mean!

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:32.979
I want to shout out our amazing field production
crews -- freelancers that we have great relationships

00:05:32.979 --> 00:05:34.729
with all over the US and the world.

00:05:34.729 --> 00:05:40.669
I want to shout out Priti Gupta, who is a
fixer who's worked with us on several India

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:45.819
videos. It's thanks to people like her that
we get to speak to people on the ground and

00:05:45.819 --> 00:05:47.900
understand what's going on at a deeper level.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.809
I’m shouting out the Styles and Standards
team, aka Vox’s copyeditors. 

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:58.550
Can you tell me if you can think of any mistakes
that our team makes over and over again?

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:03.550
I mean there are just little things like the
Vox style is not to use periods in US or DC,

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:07.159
which comes up constantly. That’s going
to be my next tattoo I think.&nbsp;

00:06:07.159 --> 00:06:11.740
So I want to shout out the Vox newsroom for
doing such an incredible job on Covid coverage

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:14.389
this year. They've just been a really invaluable
source.

00:06:14.389 --> 00:06:19.289
I want to shout out all the viewers around
the world who have contributed translations

00:06:19.289 --> 00:06:25.879
for our videos.&nbsp;Some of our videos have gotten
upwards of 70 langauge translations. I can't

00:06:25.879 --> 00:06:29.689
even name 70 languages off the top of my head,
that is phenomenal.

00:06:29.689 --> 00:06:32.300
And I wanted to thank our first subscriber.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:34.039
Was I first?

00:06:34.039 --> 00:06:37.520
Ok technically he’s our earliest remaining
subscriber.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:42.869
I was coming over to vox from the Polygon
and Verge network of Vox Media so when I heard

00:06:42.869 --> 00:06:46.949
there was a mainstream news outlet coming
from the same publisher, I was like yeah,

00:06:46.949 --> 00:06:48.669
let me get on board here right away.&nbsp;

00:06:48.669 --> 00:06:52.460
Anything that you would like to see us cover
more in future videos?

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:57.520
Reading Vox was always more of a US focused
thing, obviously, so if I had to ask, hey

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:02.099
more Canadian stories, I'd be interested in
that. But that's more of a very light wish.

00:07:02.099 --> 00:07:07.699
I guess I have to shout out all the people
who have worked at Vox and have contributed

00:07:07.699 --> 00:07:14.179
so so much to us. These people all taught
me so much during the course of my career

00:07:14.179 --> 00:07:15.179
here.&nbsp;

00:07:15.179 --> 00:07:20.192
I don’t think there’s anything else … except
to just say thank you.

00:07:20.192 --> 00:07:21.192
Thank you

00:07:21.192 --> 00:07:22.192
From the bottom of our hearts

00:07:22.192 --> 00:07:23.192
For your incredible archive

00:07:23.192 --> 00:07:24.349
For sticking with us all these years

00:07:24.349 --> 00:07:27.018
For building this tool that we use so much.&nbsp;

00:07:35.509 --> 00:07:42.169
The sheep noise that happens when you get
an error is Dave C.'s mother. Dave C. is one

00:07:42.169 --> 00:07:45.159
of the developers on the team -- he's still
on the team, in fact he's the last one of

00:07:45.159 --> 00:07:51.429
the original people who is still directly
on the team and his mother just recorded the

00:07:51.429 --> 00:07:57.369
noise with her mouth. Just "Baaa" and that's
the noise. "Baaa"

